incident bf319971-77de-4fb1-81a1-9be32f42ef16  state triaging  anchor 08:51:43
triage: 3 services, severity critical, start from frontend-proxy, 1 unmeasured edge(s) crossed

states: triaging -> planning -> investigating -> synthesizing -> proposing
trajectory: d75bbe66-1041-40e1-a918-a65d49bae1f3
triage judged: investigate (medium confidence, suspects unknown)
  Critical alert on frontend-proxy simultaneous with the load generator is not the isolated load-gen-only case, and it sits at the user-facing edge with frontend as a candidate cause. No open incidents to attribute it to, so it warrants investigation.
investigation: 2 round(s), 6 dispatch(es), 20 step(s)

=== PROPOSAL ===
  action      : revert_config (config_revert)
  target      : frontend
  expect      : Restoring frontend's pre-08:49:31 config (dnsPolicy ClusterFirst, no explicit dnsConfig) [tr_929c50e7f8bf] should let it resolve in-cluster names again. Caller-side measurement on frontend-proxy: error ratio falls from ~0.905 toward zero, and the ratio becomes defined across consecutive 5m intervals instead of dropping out on empty denominators [tr_2635c8e1c2ef].
  within      : 600s
  risk        : If the diagnosis is wrong this buys a second outage interval for nothing: the container is recreated and in-process cache discarded. Changes #3 and #4 were truncated and never examined, so a blanket revert also moves settings nobody inspected. The DNS mechanism was inferred from the change record, never witnessed in traces or logs. Reversible, which is what keeps this above abstention at medium confidence.
  blast radius: frontend directly; frontend-proxy (its inbound caller and the alerting service) and load-generator behind it see resets during the recreate.
  if wrong    : Falsified if after 10 minutes frontend-proxy's error ratio stays near 0.905 with undefined intervals. That would point at frontend never reaching Running/Ready (pod status and restart counts were never queried) or at the unmeasured edge below frontend. Note frontend's own span metrics are structurally absent [tr_a9a3db832182], so their continued absence is not evidence of failure.
  rests on    : tr_929c50e7f8bf, tr_2635c8e1c2ef
  execution   : not executed - a proposal is a claim, never the change (ADR-0028 §1)

context: 24152 pushed / 3698 pulled (pull rate 0.1328), 2 section(s) dropped

=== VERDICT ===
  fault class : bad_config
  fix class   : config_revert
  confidence  : medium
  evidence    : tr_929c50e7f8bf, tr_2635c8e1c2ef, tr_2dcc48fc8659, tr_66fc0c934048, tr_362c05685e3e, tr_a9a3db832182
  root cause  : Two minutes before onset, platform-automation rewrote the frontend pod spec's DNS settings: it removed the cluster-default `dnsPolicy: ClusterFirst` and substituted an explicit `dnsConfig` pointing at a single public resolver (8.8.8.8) [tr_929c50e7f8bf]. A public resolver cannot answer for cluster-internal service names, so frontend can neither resolve nor reach any in-cluster dependency, and the value named in that config is itself the thing that breaks every request. The downstream picture is consistent: frontend produced no spans of any kind, inbound or outbound, across the whole window [tr_2dcc48fc8659] and no log lines at all [tr_66fc0c934048], while frontend-proxy — the caller that alerted — shows an error ratio near 0.905 with intervals where its call volume disappears entirely [tr_2635c8e1c2ef]. frontend-proxy is the service that noticed, not the service that failed; its Envoy restarted cleanly at ~08:48:51 with no fatal startup output [tr_362c05685e3e], so the proxy binary and bootstrap are not the defect. The failing mechanism is a configuration value that names the wrong resolver.
  OPEN        : No direct confirmation that frontend is actually Running and Ready — pod status, restart counts and container exit reasons were never queried, so a fresh workload that simply failed to come up cannot be excluded.
  OPEN        : No DNS-failure evidence was observed anywhere: frontend emitted no outbound spans [tr_2dcc48fc8659] and frontend-proxy returned no access logs or response-flag records such as no_healthy_upstream or upstream connect failure [tr_362c05685e3e], so the resolution-failure mechanism is inferred from the change record rather than witnessed.
  OPEN        : Whether the Envoy (re)start at ~08:48:51 [tr_362c05685e3e] is a consequence of the rollout, an unrelated bring-up, or a contributor to the error ratio is unresolved; it lands between the DNS edit and onset.
  OPEN        : The two earlier changes in the set (#3 and #4) were truncated in the sample and were never examined on their own merits.
  OPEN        : Triage reported one unmeasured edge crossed; the service on the far side of it was never dispatched against, so a dependency below frontend remains unchecked.
  OPEN        : frontend-proxy's HTTP status breakdown (5xx vs 503) and upstream connection-failure counters were never retrieved [tr_2635c8e1c2ef], so the caller-side failure shape is only partly characterised.

flags: none

